/-! # Kernel of the geometric fundamental-group map at a rational boundary point

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 0BTD, 0BSW and 0BSM, in `pione.tex`
(blueprint: lem-dl-a1-pi1-kernel-inertia).

The geometric schemes are arbitrary models of the specified cartesian squares. The maps on
fundamental groups are characterized by their actual actions on finite étale fibres. Local
inertia uses the recorded henselian boundary data, without a kernel-generation assumption.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.PreGaloisCategory AlgebraicGeometry
open AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme
open scoped FintypeCatDiscrete

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.FiniteEtale

/-- Filling a rational point kills exactly the closed normal subgroup generated by its
henselian inertia in the geometric fundamental group, for every choice of local path.

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 0BTD (lemma-normal-pione-quotient-inertia), 0BSW
(lemma-identify-inertia) and 0BSM (lemma-local-exact-sequence-normal)
(blueprint: lem-dl-a1-pi1-kernel-inertia).

The cartesian squares specify C = C₀ × k and U = U₀ × k, and the two projection equations
specify their canonical open immersion. `s` is a rational closed point. `DenseRange barη`
says that its underlying point is generic. `GU` and `GC` are small profinite models of the
actual fibre-automorphism groups, identified with the FLC-stalk automorphism groups through
`exists_fiber_flc_equivalence`. The equations `hq` and `hi` specify the induced map and the
geometric inclusion by restriction to pulled-back finite étale covers; neither is arbitrary.
The subgroup appearing in the conclusion is the preimage of the local inertia image under
that inclusion, which is exactly the subgroup J_s of the node. `D.residueAction` is pinned by
reduction in the recorded boundary datum, and `D.decomposition` is pinned by `D.path`.
The final clause varies that path arbitrarily in the arithmetic fibre functor, including
paths with nontrivial arithmetic component; `hθ` pins the changed decomposition map.

Proof, following the node's nine steps:
1. Smoothness over the finite field gives regularity and normality; geometric connectedness
and nonemptiness give integrality. Finite type gives Noetherianity. The nonempty open U₀ is
normal integral, separated and finite type with the same function field. Use
`lem-weil-pbc-smooth-scheme-over-field-regular`, `lem-sc-smooth-normal`,
`lem-sc-regular-connected-integral`, `lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian` and
`lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-open-subscheme-noetherian`.
2. After scalar extension the curve C is smooth and connected, hence integral, and its
nonempty open U is connected. The arithmetic fundamental-group exact sequences identify the
geometric groups P and Q with closed normal subgroups of G₀ = π₁(U₀) and H₀ = π₁(C₀).
Use `def-sbc-smooth-morphisms`, `lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-base-change-composition`,
`lem-weil-pbc-smooth-scheme-over-field-regular`, `lem-sc-regular-connected-integral` and
`lem-wf-arithmetic-lift-fibre-path`.
3. The generic Galois presentations give surjections a : Γ_F → G₀ and b : Γ_F → H₀.
Their kernels are generated as closed normal subgroups by the strict local groups over U₀,
and by those groups together with the strict local group L_s, respectively. Functoriality
identifies b = q₀ a, so q₀ is onto. Use `lem-dl-a1-normal-integral-generic-inertia` and
`lem-galois-pi1-functoriality`.
4. The henselian decomposition/residue theorem identifies the residue kernel I_s with L_s
and the chosen decomposition homomorphism with a restricted to its decomposition group.
Consequently its image J_s is a(L_s) and lies in P; no injectivity of that map is needed.
Use `lem-dl-a1-henselian-decomposition-inertia`, `d-real-boundary`,
`lem-galois-pi1-functoriality` and `lem-sc-integral-scheme-stalks-in-function-field`.
5. Since b(L_s) = 1, J_s lies in ker q₀ and so does its closed normal closure N₀ in G₀.
For the reverse inclusion, pull N₀ back along a, use
`lem-dl-a1-normal-integral-generic-inertia` for ker b, and lift an element of ker q₀ along a.
6. The local decomposition group maps onto Gal(k/k₀): on every constant finite cover its
arithmetic action is the residue action, since s is rational. Its image D₀ normalizes J_s,
and G₀ = P D₀. Arithmetic path transport only conjugates the abelian finite-field group.
Use `lem-dl-a1-henselian-decomposition-inertia` and `lem-wf-arithmetic-lift-fibre-path`.
7. The closed normal closure M of J_s in P is normalized by D₀: conjugation preserves J_s,
and minimality applied to d and d⁻¹ gives equality. It is closed normal in G₀, so M = N₀.
8. Functoriality identifies q with q₀ restricted to P. For h in Q, lift h along q₀; its
arithmetic image is trivial, so the lift belongs to P. This proves surjectivity; continuity
comes from `lem-galois-pi1-functoriality`. Its kernel is N₀ = M.
9. A changed path conjugates J_s by g in G₀. Write g = p d with p in P and d in D₀.
As d normalizes J_s, the changed subgroup is p J_s p⁻¹ and has the same closed normal closure.
Use the path-transport clause of `d-real-boundary`.
-/
theorem piOne_kernel_inertia
    {k₀ k : Type u} [Field k₀] [Finite k₀] [Field k] [IsAlgClosed k]
    [Algebra k₀ k] [IsAlgClosure k₀ k]
    (C₀ U₀ : Over (Spec (.of k₀)))
    [IsSeparated C₀.hom] [Smooth C₀.hom]
    [LocallyOfFiniteType C₀.hom] [QuasiCompact C₀.hom]
    (hPure : C₀.left.IsPureDimension 1)
    (hConnected : ConnectedSpace (Limits.pullback C₀.hom
      (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap k₀ k))) : Scheme.{u}))
    (j₀ : U₀ ⟶ C₀) [IsOpenImmersion j₀.left]
    (x : C₀.left) (hx : IsClosed ({x} : Set C₀.left))
    (s : Spec (.of k₀) ⟶ C₀.left)
    (hs : s ≫ C₀.hom = 𝟙 (Spec (.of k₀)))
    (hsx : (Scheme.SpecToEquivOfField k₀ C₀.left s).1 = x)
    (hU₀ : Set.range j₀.left.base = ({x} : Set C₀.left)ᶜ)
    {C U : Scheme.{u}}
    (pC : C ⟶ C₀.left) (aC : C ⟶ Spec (.of k))
    (pU : U ⟶ U₀.left) (aU : U ⟶ Spec (.of k))
    (hC : IsPullback pC aC C₀.hom
      (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap k₀ k))))
    (hU : IsPullback pU aU U₀.hom
      (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap k₀ k))))
    (j : U ⟶ C) (hj : j ≫ pC = pU ≫ j₀.left) (hjk : j ≫ aC = aU)
    {Ω : Type u} [Field Ω] [IsAlgClosed Ω]
    (barη : Spec (.of Ω) ⟶ U) (hGeneric : DenseRange barη.base)
    (D : HenselianBoundaryLocalData j₀.left (barη ≫ pU) x)
    (GU GC : ProfiniteGrp.{u})
    (eU : GU ≃ₜ* Aut (fiber barη))
    (eC : GC ≃ₜ* Aut (fiber (barη ≫ j)))
    (q : GU →* GC)
    (hq : ∀ g : GU, eC (q g) =
      (pullbackFiberIso j barη).symm ≪≫
        Functor.isoWhiskerLeft (FiniteEtale.pullback j) (eU g) ≪≫ pullbackFiberIso j barη)
    (i : GU →* Aut (fiber (barη ≫ pU)))
    (hi : ∀ g : GU, i g =
      (pullbackFiberIso pU barη).symm ≪≫
        Functor.isoWhiskerLeft (FiniteEtale.pullback pU) (eU g) ≪≫
          pullbackFiberIso pU barη) :
    Continuous q ∧ Function.Surjective q ∧
      q.ker = (Subgroup.normalClosure
        (((D.residueAction.ker.map D.decomposition).comap i : Subgroup GU) : Set GU)
          ).topologicalClosure ∧
      ∀ (γ : fiber (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap D.K D.Ω)) ≫
          D.localGenericPoint) ≅ fiber (barη ≫ pU))
        (θ : (D.Ω ≃ₐ[D.K] D.Ω) →* Aut (fiber (barη ≫ pU)))
        (hθ : ∀ (σ : D.Ω ≃ₐ[D.K] D.Ω) (P : U₀.left.FiniteEtale)
          (z : (fiber (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap D.K D.Ω)) ≫
            D.localGenericPoint)).obj P),
          (γ.inv.app P ((θ σ).hom.app P (γ.hom.app P z))).left =
            Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom σ.toRingHom) ≫ z.left),
        (Subgroup.normalClosure
          (((D.residueAction.ker.map θ).comap i : Subgroup GU) : Set GU)).topologicalClosure =
          (Subgroup.normalClosure
            (((D.residueAction.ker.map D.decomposition).comap i : Subgroup GU) : Set GU)
              ).topologicalClosure := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.FiniteEtale
